### Gate B — FAIL

The HumanEval contract quantifies over every `N >= 2` permutation grid and
every positive `k`. `SPEC.target-examples` covers only four concrete input
pairs. It therefore does not establish the full intended input domain, even
though it includes both prompt examples and additional corner/alternation
cases.

For the implementation argument, uniqueness of value `1` forces the first path
cell to be `1`. The second cell is the least orthogonal neighbor of `1`; that
neighbor can return to `1`, which is globally least, so the lexicographically
least path alternates between those two values. This argument and the
independent differential tests support intent alignment, but they are not a
universal K theorem.

